## Part Two Principles

#### Article 2

The implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection shall be carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;  
b. justice;  
c. substantive equality;  
d. participatory;  
e. non-discrimination;  
f. best interests of the Child;  
g. good governance; and  
h. sustainability.

## Part Three Intent and Objectives

#### Article 3

This Regional Regulation is intended as a reference or guideline in governance, development, and community services in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area.

#### Article 4

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. realize Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area that is effective, efficient, optimal, transparent, and accountable, as well as administratively orderly;  
b. realize Gender Responsive development by integrating Gender Perspective into planning, budgeting, implementation, monitoring, evaluation, supervision, and reporting on policies, programs, activities and sub-activities of development in the Regency Area;  
c. realize Gender Equality in family, community, national and state life;  
d. realize Gender Responsive regional budget management;  
e. support efforts to increase the capacity of forums, institutions and/or organizations for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;  
f. increase the synergy of the Regency Regional Government and the community in the development of Gender Responsive Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and  
g. increase Community Participation in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area.

## Part Four Scope

#### Article 5

The scope of this Regional Regulation regulates:
a. Quality of Life of Women;  
b. Protection of Women;  
c. Protection of Children;  
d. Improvement of Family Quality;  
e. Gender and Child Data System;  
f. cooperation;  
g. Community Participation;  
h. coordination;  
i. monitoring;  
j. reporting;  
k. awards;  
l. funding; and  
m. sanctions.

# CHAPTER II QUALITY OF LIFE OF WOMEN

## Part One General

#### Article 6

1. The Regency Regional Government has the authority to implement Quality of Life of Women in the Regency Area.  
2. The implementation of Quality of Life of Women in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be carried out by the Regency Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

## Part Two Implementation

### Paragraph 1 General

#### Article 7

The implementation of Quality of Life of Women in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 6 paragraph (1), consists of:
a. implementation of PUG in the Regency Area;  
b. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus;  
c. Women's Empowerment in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in Community Organizations in the Regency Area;  
d. strengthening and development of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area; and  
e. Capacity Building of Women in the Regency Area.

### Paragraph 2 Implementation of PUG in the Regency Area

#### Article 8

1. The Regent is responsible for the implementation of PUG in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 7 letter a.  
2. The responsibility of the Regent as referred to in paragraph (1), may be delegated to the Vice Regent.  
3. In the implementation of PUG in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent shall designate the Regency Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection as the coordinator for the implementation of PUG in the Regency Area.  
4. Further provisions regarding the implementation of PUG in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be regulated by a separate Regional Regulation.

### Paragraph 3 Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus

#### Article 9

1. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, shall be implemented through:
   a. fulfillment of PUG Institutionalization standards;  
   b. integration of Gender Perspective;  
   c. preparation of planning and budgeting; and  
   d. advocacy, facilitation, and provision of technical assistance.  
2. Integration of Gender Perspective as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, shall be incorporated into the policies and development programs of the Regency Area.  
3. The policies and development programs of the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (2), consist of:
   a. RPJPD;  
   b. RPJMD;  
   c. RKPD;  
   d. Renstra; and  
   e. Renja.  
4. The policies and development programs of the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (2), include:
   a. planning;  
   b. formulation;  
   c. implementation;  
   d. monitoring;  
   e. control; and  
   f. evaluation.  
5. The preparation of planning and budgeting as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, shall consider equal Access, Participation, control, and Benefits between women and men, taking into account the aspirations, needs, and specific problems of women and men.  
6. Advocacy, facilitation and provision of technical assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, shall be directed to institutions that have legislative functions and vertical agencies in the Regency Area related to the implementation of PUG.

#### Article 10

1. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.  
2. The PUG Institutionalization standards as referred to in paragraph (1), consist of:
   a. commitment of the Regent;  
   b. policies and programs;  
   c. institutions;  
   d. resources;  
   e. Sex-Disaggregated Data and Gender statistics;  
   f. implementation methods; and  
   g. Community Participation.  
3. The PUG Institutionalization procedures as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
   a. coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency Area;  
   b. coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency Area;  
   c. policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency Area including PPRG under the authority of the Regency Area; and  
   d. socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency Area including PPRG under the authority of the Regency Area.  
4. Coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a, includes:
   a. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations, legal products of the Regency Area, and operational policies regarding PUG; and  
   b. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja as well as Gender Responsive budgets of the Regency Regional Apparatus.  
5. Coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b, includes:
   a. establishment of PUG Working Group;  
   b. preparation of annual and 5 (five) year PUG Working Group action plans;  
   c. PUG Working Group coordination meetings; and  
   d. integration of Sex-Disaggregated Data into the Gender and Child Data System.  
6. Policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter c, includes:
   a. technical coordination meetings for PUG implementation;  
   b. technical guidance and assistance for PPRG in the preparation of Gender Responsive programs, activities, and budgets; and  
   c. facilitation in the preparation of Gender Responsive Regency Area policies.  
7. Socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter d, includes:
   a. dissemination of policies for PUG and PPRG implementation to stakeholders; and  
   b. strengthening of Community Participation in PUG implementation.

