## Part Three Fulfillment of Children's Rights

### Paragraph 1 General

#### Article 52

1. The Regency Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility in the implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights.  
2. The Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
   a. the right to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and dignity, and receive protection from violence and Discrimination;  
   b. the right to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;  
   c. the right to worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians;  
   d. the right to know their Parents, be raised, and be cared for by their own Parents;  
   e. the right to be cared for or adopted as a foster Child or adopted Child by others in the event that for any reason the Parents cannot guarantee the growth and development of the Child, or the Child is in a state of neglect;  
   f. the right to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;  
   g. the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing their personality and level of intelligence in accordance with their interests and talents;  
   h. the right to receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, education personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;  
   i. the right to express and have their opinion heard, receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for their self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;  
   j. the right to rest and utilize leisure time, socialize with children of the same age, play, recreate, and create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;  
   k. the right to receive protection from Discrimination, Exploitation, both economic and sexual, neglect, cruelty, violence, and abuse, injustice, and other mistreatment while in the care of Parents, Guardians, or any other party responsible for care;  
   l. the right to be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are valid reasons and/or legal rules showing that the separation and/or divorce is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;  
   m. the right to meet directly and have regular personal contact with both Parents, receive care, maintenance, education and protection for the growth and development process from both Parents in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests, obtain living expenses from both Parents, and obtain other Children's Rights, in the event of separation and/or divorce as referred to in letter l;  
   n. the right to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare, and sexual crimes;  
   o. the right to obtain protection from being the target of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment, and to obtain freedom in accordance with the law;  
   p. the right to be treated humanely and placed separately from adults, obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal efforts, and defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial juvenile court in a closed session to the public, in the case of a Child who is deprived of liberty;  
   q. the right to confidentiality in the case of a Child who is a victim or perpetrator of sexual Violence or who is in conflict with the law;  
   r. the right to receive legal assistance and other assistance in the event that a child becomes a victim or perpetrator of a criminal act; and
   s. the right to receive protection and maintenance after divorce.  
3. The implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is carried out in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of Children.

#### Article 53

The Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency as referred to in Article 52, consists of:

a. institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency; and
b. strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Children's Lives in the Regency.

#### Article 54

The institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency as referred to in Article 53 letter a is implemented through:

a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in efforts to prevent violations of Children's rights in government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency;  
b. increasing the participation and role of Children's Forums or the like in the Regency;  
c. strengthening the institutions and resources of government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency;  
d. strengthening networks among government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency; and
e. strengthening cross-institutional cooperation among government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions to realize Child-Friendly Regency, Child-Friendly Sub-district, and Child-Friendly Village/Sub-district.

#### Article 55

1. The institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency as referred to in Article 53 letter a is implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.  
2. The standards for institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.  
3. The procedures for institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
   a. policy advocacy and assistance for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency, including:
      1. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations and legal products of the Regency related to Children's rights and efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights;  
      2. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja documents with a Children's rights perspective;  
      3. technical coordination meetings for the implementation of policies, strategies and programs, as well as monitoring and evaluation of efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights;  
      4. technical guidance and assistance in the preparation of programs, activities, and budgets that are responsive to Children's Rights and their implementation;  
      5. facilitation in the preparation of Regency policies that are responsive to Children's Rights and their implementation;  
      6. dissemination of policies on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights to stakeholders; and
      7. strengthening Community Participation in efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights; and
   b. coordination and synchronization of the Institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency, including:
      1. establishment of working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights;  
      2. preparation of work plans for working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights;  
      3. coordination meetings to strengthen working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights; and
      4. integration of data on the situation and condition of Children by gender in the Fulfillment of Children's Rights into a data system that can be accessed through official publication media.

