#### Article 56

1. The achievement of institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government institutions in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 53 letter a is measured by criteria.  
2. The criteria for achieving institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
   a. effectiveness of policies that are responsive to Children's Rights;  
   b. value and ranking of the Regency Area in the cluster of Fulfillment of Children's Rights that receives the predicate of Child-Friendly;  
   c. index of fulfillment of Children's Rights;  
   d. Child Protection Index;  
   e. number of sub-districts with the predicate of Child-Friendly sub-district in the cluster of Fulfillment of Children's Rights; and  
   f. number of villages and urban villages (kelurahan) with the ranking of Child-Friendly village/urban village (kelurahan) in the cluster of Fulfillment of Children's Rights.

#### Article 57

Strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 53 letter b is implemented through:
 a. capacity building of resources of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area;  
 b. assistance in the implementation of services for improving the quality of life of Children in the Regency Area;  
 c. development of materials and implementation of communication, information, and education on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights for Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area;  
 d. strengthening networks among Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area;  
 e. facilitation of the implementation of standardization of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area; and  
 f. development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area.

#### Article 58

1. Strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 53 letter b is implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.  
2. Standards for strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.  
3. Procedures for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
   a. provision of services for improving the quality of life of Children in the Regency Area;  
   b. coordination and synchronization of the implementation of assistance for improving the quality of life of Children in the Regency Area;  
   c. development of communication, information, and education on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights for Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area; and  
   d. strengthening networks among Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area.

#### Article 59

1. The achievement of strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 53 letter b is measured by criteria.  
2. Criteria for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
   a. number of service provider institutions that meet standards; and  
   b. percentage of Children served in the Fulfillment of Children's Rights.

#### Article 60

Further provisions regarding the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 52 through Article 59, are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

## Part Six Special Protection of Children

### Paragraph 1 General

#### Article 61

Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area is the obligation and responsibility of the Regency Regional Government.

#### Article 62

1. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61, is provided to:
   a. Children in emergency situations;  
   b. Children in conflict with the law;  
   c. Children from minority and isolated groups;  
   d. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;  
   e. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;  
   f. Children who are victims of pornography;  
   g. Children with HIV and AIDS;  
   h. Children victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;  
   i. Children victims of bullying;  
   j. Children victims of sexual crimes;  
   k. Children victims of terrorism networks;  
   l. Children with disabilities;  
   m. Children victims of abuse and neglect;  
   n. Children with deviant social behavior; and  
   o. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.  
2. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out through efforts:
   a. rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;  
   b. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;  
   c. provision of social assistance for Children from underprivileged Families; and  
   d. provision of protection and assistance in every judicial process.  
3. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided at UPTD/UPTD PPA and Puspaga.  
4. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

#### Article 63

In addition to being obligated and responsible for implementing Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61 and Article 62, the Regency Regional Government is obligated to organize:
 a. protection of Children in Conflict;  
 b. protection of Children as Witnesses and/or Victims;  
 c. Protection of Children from acts of KGB in disasters; and  
 d. protection of Children with disabilities.

#### Article 64

Provisions regarding the protection of Women in Conflict as referred to in Article 23 through Article 25 apply mutatis mutandis to the protection of children in Conflict as referred to in Article 63 letter a.

#### Article 65

Provisions regarding the protection of Women as Witnesses and/or Victims as referred to in Article 27 through Article 29 apply mutatis mutandis to the protection of children as Witnesses and/or Victims as referred to in Article 63 letter b.

#### Article 66

Provisions regarding the protection of women from acts of KGB in Disasters as referred to in Article 31 and Article 32 apply mutatis mutandis to the protection of children from acts of KGB in Disasters as referred to in Article 63 letter c.

#### Article 67

Provisions regarding the rights of women with Disabilities as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (2), apply mutatis mutandis to the rights of children with disabilities.

#### Article 68

In addition to obtaining rights as persons with disabilities as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (2) and Article 67, Children with disabilities have the right:
 a. to receive Special Protection from Discrimination, neglect, harassment, Exploitation, as well as violence and sexual crimes;  
 b. to receive care and upbringing from Family or substitute Family to grow and develop optimally;  
 c. to have their interests protected in decision-making;  
 d. humane treatment of Children in accordance with the dignity and Rights of Children;  
 e. fulfillment of special needs;  
 f. equal treatment with other Children to achieve social integration and individual development; and  
 g. to receive social assistance.

#### Article 69

The Regency Regional Government in implementing the organization of Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61, provides:
 a. social workers and social welfare personnel;  
 b. competent and trained health personnel;  
 c. officers …c. spiritual/worship guidance officers;  
 d. educators and education personnel; and/or  
 e. legal aid personnel.

### Paragraph 2 Organization

#### Article 70

The organization of Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61, consists of:
 a. prevention of KTA involving parties in the Regency Area;  
 b. provision of services for AMPK that require coordination in the Regency Area; and  
 c. strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for AMPK in the Regency Area.

